# CODEOWNERS — requires review from the listed owners on changes to release-critical paths.
#
# This ONLY gates merges if "Require review from Code Owners" is enabled in main's branch
# protection rule (Settings -> Branches -> main -> Require a pull request before merging ->
# Require review from Code Owners). Without that setting enabled, this file only suggests
# reviewers in the PR UI and enforces nothing — see docs/release-setup.md.
#
# Replace @coinspect/dappfence-admins with the actual maintainer team handle once created. A team
# is preferred over individual users so membership changes don't require editing this file.

# This file protects itself. Without this line, a single PR could both remove a restriction
# below and exploit it, needing only an ordinary (non-admin) reviewer.
.github/CODEOWNERS                @coinspect/dappfence-admins

# The release workflow itself: what the verify/publish jobs check and do, which key(s) it
# trusts (RELEASE_SIGNING_KEYS repo variable, admin-Settings-only to edit — see
# docs/release-setup.md), and which environment/permissions gate the actual npm publish.
.github/workflows/release.yml     @coinspect/dappfence-admins

# What gets published, and how versions are allowed to move. Package manifests themselves are
# release-critical too, not just the allowlist/script: `name` and `version` are the identity npm
# registers the upload under, the `files` allow-list decides what ends up inside the tarball, and
# `dependencies` is what consumers then install. Their `test` script also runs in the test job.
# sync-versions.js writes versions into these files directly, so without this line the release
# gate could be bypassed by just editing a package.json by hand.
#
# Note lifecycle scripts no longer run while the npm credential is reachable: publish uploads
# prebuilt tarballs with --ignore-scripts and has no checkout at all. See "Why the pipeline is
# four jobs" in docs/release-setup.md.
packages/publish.json             @coinspect/dappfence-admins
packages/*/package.json           @coinspect/dappfence-admins
scripts/sync-versions.js          @coinspect/dappfence-admins

# The root manifest, anchored with a leading slash so this means the root one specifically.
# It is release-critical for a different reason than the package manifests above: `check` and
# `test` are defined here and are what the test job runs to decide a release is fit to ship, and
# `build:prod`/`build` is what produces the published @dappfence/core bundle. Editing any of them
# is arbitrary code execution inside the release, so without this line it would need only an
# ordinary reviewer.
/package.json                     @coinspect/dappfence-admins

# Build and tooling code that executes during a release. Splitting the workflow keeps this out
# of the job holding the npm credential, so none of it can mint publish authority any more --
# but it still runs in the build job and still decides what ends up inside the tarballs.
# vite.config.js shapes the published @dappfence/core bundle directly; scripts/ runs during
# `npm run check` and the build.
packages/*/vite.config.js         @coinspect/dappfence-admins
scripts/                          @coinspect/dappfence-admins
/eslint.config.js                 @coinspect/dappfence-admins

# Describes the policy the paths above enforce; drift here is a documentation-trust problem,
# not just a typo.
docs/release-setup.md             @coinspect/dappfence-admins

# Lockfile: unreviewed changes here are a common way a supply-chain attack slips in.
package-lock.json                 @coinspect/dappfence-admins
